<BaseLayout 
  title="About" 
  description="About Human System — a developing body of thought about understanding and intervening in human systems when the right action depends on context."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    
    <h2>What Human System is</h2>
    
    <p>
      Human System is the working home for an evolving body of thought about 
      understanding and intervening responsibly in human systems when the 
      right action depends on context.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      The subject is not consulting itself, though the recurring entrypoint 
      is consulting and technical or organizational work. The subject is 
      broader: how to understand a human system you have entered, and how 
      to act in it, when the correct action depends on the situation and 
      the situation can never be fully known.
    </p>
    
    <h2>Why this work exists</h2>
    
    <p>
      Experience in organizational and technical work repeatedly surfaces 
      the same tension: the expectation to provide answers because of prior 
      experience, alongside the recognition that each situation is 
      sufficiently unique that automatic prescriptions fail.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      This work attempts to hold both truths:
    </p>
    
    <ul>
      <li>
        Experience does provide patterns and better questions. It is not 
        useless.
      </li>
      <li>
        Experience becomes dangerous when recognition of a familiar pattern 
        replaces investigation of the actual context.
      </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>Perspective</h2>
    
    <p>
      The thinking develops from a specific perspective:
    </p>
    
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Consulting and technical work.</strong> The author's 
        entrypoint is organizational and technical interventions — entering 
        systems that existed before you and will continue after you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Existing theory as challenger.</strong> Established work 
        from systems thinking, socio-technical systems, complexity, 
        cybernetics, organizational learning, and psychology is used to 
        test and challenge the thinking, not merely to decorate it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence over certainty.</strong> Conclusions may change 
        as observations and evidence accumulate. The work is comfortable 
        saying that ideas are being tested and revised.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No universal claims.</strong> The thinking avoids turning 
        observations into a branded framework, method, or maturity model 
        before they have been tested.
      </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>What is in the repository</h2>
    
    <p>
      The repository at <a href="https://github.com/lagebj/human-system">github.com/lagebj/human-system</a> 
      contains:
    </p>
    
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Conceptual sources</strong> — the current stance and 
        contextual-intervention thinking, held as provisional working 
        context.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Field patterns</strong> — applied patterns from particular 
        experience, including the Trojan Horse Movement Pattern for 
        modernization in place.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An existing book manuscript</strong> — preserved as one 
        possible future synthesis. It is not the primary output and is 
        not being actively edited.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Papers</strong> — shorter, narrower publications that 
        test specific propositions.
      </li>
    </ul>
    
    <h2>Publication boundary</h2>
    
    <p>
      Being present in the public GitHub repository does not mean a piece 
      of material has been deliberately published to this website. The 
      site includes only material deliberately moved into the published 
      collection. Working material remains in the repository but is not 
      exposed here.
    </p>
    
